The reduced Planck constant, also known as the Dirac constant, is a fundamental physical constant that relates the energy of a photon to its angular frequency (

E = ω

) and momentum to its wave vector (

p = k

). It represents the quantum of action and is crucial in defining the scale at which quantum mechanical effects become significant. Its value is approximately 1.054 10^-34 Js.
